   "answer": "## Results\n\n**Template:** GenBank J01695.2, E. coli rrnB operon, 7258 bp, linear (your answer to the topology question). The 16S gene starts at position 1268, as you gave.\n\n**Simulation setting:** Both simulations used a minimum 3' match of 13 bp. The simulation output logs this value. I had requested 15 bp, but the harness kept 13 bp. A 13 bp match is less strict, so a 15 bp setting could give fewer binding sites. I did not run the 15 bp case.\n\n### 1. Products on J01695.2\n\n1. **Pair 1** (CCTACGGGNGGCWGCAG / GACTACHVGGGTATCTAATCC): 1 product.\n   - Product length: 465 bp.\n   - Positions: 1608 to 2072, top strand.\n   - The forward primer binds the top strand. The reverse primer binds the bottom strand.\n2. **Pair 2** (AGAGTTTGATCMTGGC / CCGTCAATTCMTTTGAGTTT): 1 product.\n   - Product length: 919 bp.\n   - Positions: 1275 to 2193, top strand.\n   - The forward primer binds the top strand. The reverse primer binds the bottom strand.\n\n### 2. Forward primer start in the 16S gene\n\nThe position is the template start minus 1268, plus 1. The calculation tool gave these values.\n\n- **Pair 1 forward** (CCTACGGGNGGCWGCAG): starts at position 341 in the 16S gene (template position 1608).\n- **Pair 2 forward** (AGAGTTTGATCMTGGC): starts at position 8 in the 16S gene (template position 1275).\n\nPair 2 forward is a common primer name in the literature. Check that your naming convention matches the start position found here. I did not run a tool to check the literature name.\n\n### 3. Melting temperature of CCTACGGGAGGCAGCAG\n\n- **Melting temperature (Tm): 61.39 °C.**\n- Method: Primer3 (SantaLucia 1998), salt correction SantaLucia.\n- Conditions: Na+ 50 mM, Mg2+ 1.5 mM, dNTP 0.6 mM, primer 200 nM.\n- Length: 17 nt. GC content: 70.59%.\n- I chose this method because you did not name one. The harness settings set the conditions.\n- The Tm is not the annealing temperature. Use the polymerase vendor rule for the annealing temperature.\n\nOther values from the same call (not asked): hairpin Tm 38.96 °C, hairpin ΔG −0.11 kcal/mol, self-dimer ΔG −3.48 kcal/mol.\n\n## What is uncertain\n\n- The product counts come from an in-silico check with a 13 bp 3' match. A real PCR can give products that this check does not show. The check does not search other genomes.\n- The simulation treated the degenerate positions as matches. I did not test each possible sequence of the degenerate primers.\n- I calculated the Tm for the unambiguous primer only. I did not calculate the Tm of the degenerate primers.\n- My first Tm call failed because I sent the primer in the wrong format. The second call, in the correct format, succeeded.\n\n## What waits for you\n\n1. Confirm the minimum 3' match: 13 bp (the value in use) or 15 bp (your request). I can run the check at 15 bp if you want.\n2. Confirm the Tm method and salt correction you want to report.\n3. Confirm your naming convention for the pair 2 forward primer. Its start position here is 8.\n\nSettings used, from the decision record: Monovalent cations (Na+ and K+) in the PCR buffer, mM: 50 · Mg2+ in the PCR buffer, mM: 1.5 · Total dNTP in the PCR, mM: 0.6 · Primer concentration, nM: 200 · Melting temperature method: primer3 · Salt correction of the Tm: santalucia · Shortest 3' match for a primer binding site, bp: 13 · Linear or circular template: linear.",
